Then a `GET /products/search?q=...` route in `routes/products.js`, same pagination shape as the existing listing:

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const q = (req.query.q || "").trim();
  if (!q) return res.json([]);
  const page = Math.max(1, parseInt(req.query.page, 10) || 1);
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents,
            ts_rank(search_vector, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)) AS rank
     FROM products
     WHERE active AND search_vector @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)
     ORDER BY rank DESC
     LIMIT 50 OFFSET $2`,
    [q, (page - 1) * 50]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

The one real fork: full-text search (above) gives relevance ranking and stemming ("running" matches "run") but no partial-word or typo tolerance. If fuzzy/substring matching on things like SKUs or typos matters more than ranking, `pg_trgm` with a GIN index and `%` similarity is the alternative — simpler, but no relevance score. Left out: query debouncing/caching on the client and any autocomplete-as-you-type behavior, since those are frontend/product decisions, not schema ones.

